Task overview - Objective: Identify all plausible actors matching six clues: born in the 1960s; early-life technician job; widespread recognition via a well-known 2001 film; career starting with a minor role in a 1988 film; credits in both British and Hollywood productions; and an interview sentiment that success means freedom to choose and doing things from desire, not obligation. Provide a transparent, evidence-backed report with citations, note uncertainties and near-misses, and conclude with a likelihood ranking and final answer (full name). Summary of working history - Planning: Translated the clues into verifiable criteria (birth decade; technician job; 1988 minor film role; 2001 breakthrough; UK and Hollywood credits; interview quote/close paraphrase). - Web research execution: Conducted a comprehensive web-sourced investigation synthesizing reputable sources (BBC, The Guardian, Wikipedia with corroboration, India Today, The Hindu, Jagranjosh). The findings consistently surfaced one primary candidate (Irrfan Khan) and several near-miss candidates. - Results consolidation: Compiled structured evidence for Irrfan Khan across all six clues, including citations and direct/close paraphrase quotes about choosing roles out of conviction. Identified and documented near-miss candidates and why they fail key criteria. Noted minor inconsistencies (e.g., birth year variation in one source; spelling of birth name; absence of a verbatim match to the “success means freedom to choose” phrasing, but strong thematic alignment in interviews). Primary plausible candidate - Candidate: Irrfan Khan - Full name and birth date/year: - Sahabzade Irfan Ali Khan (professionally “Irrfan Khan”); born 7 January 1967. - Sources: - BBC obituary: “He was born Sahabzada Irfan Ali Khan on 7 January, 1967...” https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - Wikipedia (biographical overview): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irrfan_Khan - Early-life technician job: - Worked as an air conditioner repairman in Mumbai in his early days; widely reported anecdote includes visiting Rajesh Khanna’s home for AC work. - Sources: - Jagranjosh biography: https://www.jagranjosh.com/general-knowledge/irrfan-khan-biography-1588144500-1 - Wikipedia Early life (with references): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irrfan_Khan - Note: Direct top-tier outlet citation of the AC-repairman job was not captured in-session, but the detail is consistently reported across biographies and Wikipedia references. - 1988 minor role (career start): - Salaam Bombay! (1988, Mira Nair) — cast in a small role; his part was reduced/ended up on the cutting room floor. - Sources: - BBC: “Cast as one of the younger characters in Mira Nair’s Oscar nominated Salaam Bombay!, he was devastated when his character hit the cutting room floor.” https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - The Guardian: “Khan’s part ended up on the cutting-room floor.” https://www.theguardian.com/film/2013/jul/25/irrfan-khan-bollywood-d-day - India Today retrospective: https://www.indiatoday.in/movies/bollywood/story/re-watching-irrfan-from-salaam-bombay-to-angrezi-medium-1672387-2020-04-29 - 2001 well-known film leading to widespread recognition: - The Warrior (2001; British-Indian film directed by Asif Kapadia) — widely cited as his breakthrough; BAFTA-winning Best British Film; launched his international career. - Sources: - BBC: “His breakthrough came in the British-Indian film The Warrior... The critical success of The Warrior launched his film career.” https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - The Guardian profile: recognition centered on The Warrior (2001). https://www.theguardian.com/film/2013/jul/25/irrfan-khan-bollywood-d-day - Wikipedia (year/awards context): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Warrior_(2001_British_film) - Note: The Warrior is commonly listed as a 2001 British film (festival-year conventions). - Credits in British and Hollywood productions: - British/international examples: The Warrior (2001); A Mighty Heart (2007, Michael Winterbottom); Slumdog Millionaire (2008, Danny Boyle); The Namesake (2006, Mira Nair). BBC overview: https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - Hollywood studio films: The Amazing Spider-Man (2012, Sony/Columbia); Life of Pi (2012, Fox); Jurassic World (2015, Universal); Inferno (2016, Sony). BBC overview and Wikipedia filmography: https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irrfan_Khan - Interview stance aligning with “success = freedom to choose/desire over obligation”: - Thematic matches: - BBC: “By now, Khan had reached the stage where he could be choosy about the roles he took on. ‘I try to do films which leave a longer impact... I prefer movies which have a longer relationship.’” https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - The Guardian: “I did try to do it but it made me feel empty. I cannot do things which don’t come naturally to me… you have to stick to your convictions and stand by your plus points.” https://www.theguardian.com/film/2013/jul/25/irrfan-khan-bollywood-d-day - Commentary: While not verbatim to the provided phrasing, these reputable sources strongly reflect the sentiment that his success allowed him to choose roles based on desire/conviction rather than obligation. - Career span: - From the 1988 film credit through the 1990s–2010s, spanning nearly three decades. Sources: BBC obituary; Wikipedia filmography. Uncertainties and notes - Birth year discrepancy: BBC/Wikipedia list 1967; Jagranjosh lists 1966. Given BBC/Wikipedia alignment and general consensus, 1967 is preferred; discrepancy noted. - Birth name spelling: BBC uses “Sahabzada,” while some sources (e.g., Wikipedia at times) show “Sahabzade.” This is a transliteration variance; the professional name “Irrfan Khan” is consistent. - Technician-job sourcing: AC-repairman detail is widely reported but not captured in-session from a top-tier outlet; nonetheless consistent across biographies and Wikipedia references. - 2001 release-year conventions: The Warrior’s festival-year (2001) is the customary reference; wide releases vary by territory. - 1988 “minor role” nuance: His part in Salaam Bombay! was cut/reduced; still widely cited as his earliest film involvement, consistent with the clue. Near-miss candidates (do not fully meet the criteria) - Kay Kay Menon (born 1966): No documented 1988 film bit-part; recognition not specifically tied to a 2001 film; no technician-job evidence; limited Hollywood studio credits. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kay_Kay_Menon - Vijay Raaz (born 1963): Monsoon Wedding visibility (2001/2002), but “widespread recognition” later; no 1988 minor film role; no technician evidence; limited Hollywood studio credits. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vijay_Raaz - Adil Hussain (born 1963): International recognition mainly from 2012; no 1988 film bit-part; no technician evidence. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adil_Hussain - Rahul Bose (born 1967): Recognition circa 2002; no 1988 film bit-part; no technician evidence; limited Hollywood studio credits. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rahul_Bose - John Hannah (born 1962): Early electrician apprenticeship (technician-adjacent) and UK/Hollywood credits, but breakthrough was 1994, not 2001; no 1988 minor film start. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Hannah_(actor) Likelihood ranking - High likelihood: Irrfan Khan — cleanly matches all six clues with strong corroboration. - Medium/low likelihood: The near-miss candidates above — each fails one or more key criteria. Key citations - BBC obituary (birth, 1988 cut role, 2001 breakthrough, UK/Hollywood credits, “choosy” quote): https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-43476602 - The Guardian profile/interview (Salaam Bombay! cut, The Warrior breakthrough, convictions/choice philosophy): https://www.theguardian.com/film/2013/jul/25/irrfan-khan-bollywood-d-day - Wikipedia overview (biography/filmography, AC-repairman detail with references): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irrfan_Khan - India Today retrospective (1988 role reduction): https://www.indiatoday.in/movies/bollywood/story/re-watching-irrfan-from-salaam-bombay-to-angrezi-medium-1672387-2020-04-29 - Jagranjosh biography (AC-repairman detail): https://www.jagranjosh.com/general-knowledge/irrfan-khan-biography-1588144500-1 - The Warrior (2001) page (year/awards context): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Warrior_(2001_British_film) Final answer \boxed{Sahabzade Irfan Ali Khan (Irrfan Khan)}
