LLM extracted final answer: **Step-by-step Analysis:** 1. The clues describe an actor born in the 1960s who: • Worked as a technician early in life • Had a minor film role in 1988 • Achieved major recognition with a 2001 film • Has credits in both British and Hollywood productions • Expressed that success is the freedom to choose roles out of desire rather than obligation 2. From the Agent Summary: • Irrfan Khan (born 7 January 1967) matches the birth-decade criterion. • Before acting, he worked as an air-conditioner repairman (a technician job). • His first film appearance was a small part (largely cut) in “Salaam Bombay!” (1988). • He gained international recognition with the BAFTA-winning 2001 film “The Warrior”. • His career includes British films (e.g., “The Warrior”) and Hollywood titles (“Life of Pi”, “Jurassic World”, “The Amazing Spider-Man”). • Interviews quoted in BBC and The Guardian convey that for him success means being free to choose roles he truly wants—mirroring the stated sentiment. 3. No other candidate in the summary satisfies all clues simultaneously; near-miss candidates fail at least one key requirement. Therefore, the actor is Irrfan Khan. **Final Answer:** \boxed{Irrfan Khan} **Confidence:** 83 **Supporting Evidence:** Matches every clue: 1967 birth (1960s), early A/C technician work, 1988 debut in “Salaam Bombay!”, breakthrough in 2001’s “The Warrior”, roles in British and Hollywood films, and interview statements about valuing freedom of choice in work. **Potential Weaknesses:** The question’s phrase “full name” could be interpreted as the birth name “Sahabzade Irfan Ali Khan”; however, guidelines favor the commonly used first+last form unless explicitly requesting middle names, making “Irrfan Khan” the safer formatted answer.
